# Burton AI

Burton is a local coding agent for projects. It uses Python, Ollama and a model that runs on your machine. It can inspect and edit files, run tests and commands, and keep a conversation history.

## Requirements

- Windows and Python 3.12 or newer.
- Ollama installed and running.
- A model downloaded in Ollama. The default is `qwen2.5-coder:7b`; change it with `--model` or `/models`.

## Install and interactive use

From the Burton folder:

```powershell
py -3.12 -m pip install -r requirements.txt
ollama pull qwen2.5-coder:7b
py -3.12 .\burton.py --root .
```

`--root` is the project the agent works on. If omitted, Burton uses the current folder. To launch Burton from anywhere, call `burton.py` with its full path and pass the working folder in `--root`.

## Skills

Skills live in `skills/*.md`. `core` skills are always active and cannot be disabled; `optional` skills can be toggled:

```text
/skills
/skills on git
/skills off python
/skills only git,testing
```

Changes apply on the next turn. Default optional skills are `auto-skills`, `explore`, `clean-code`, `python`, `testing`, `spanish`, `self-review`, `terminal-apps` and `debugging`. The `auto-skills` skill is active by default: it lists the optional skills in the prompt and lets the model load what it needs with `@skills id,id` for that turn only. The `spanish` skill makes Burton speak Spanish while code, comments, filenames, documentation and saved files stay in English.

Each skill is capped at 1200 characters and optional skills share a 7000-character budget. If the budget is full, lower-priority skills are skipped; `/skills` marks them as `over budget`.

## External interface

The external interface sends a task to the same worker from scripts or other agents. Use one operation per invocation and keep the same `--root`:

```powershell
py -3.12 C:\path\to\burton_ai\burton.py --root C:\path\to\project --talk "Review the tests and fix the failure"
py -3.12 C:\path\to\burton_ai\burton.py --root C:\path\to\project --status
py -3.12 C:\path\to\burton_ai\burton.py --root C:\path\to\project --response
py -3.12 C:\path\to\burton_ai\burton.py --root C:\path\to\project --wait --timeout 120
```

- `--talk TEXT` starts the turn in the background and returns JSON with its id.
- `--status` returns JSON state, pending approval and whether the agent is thinking.
- `--response` returns the text available so far.
- `--wait` waits for the end and returns the final response; `--timeout` limits the wait.
- `--stop` requests cancelling the active turn.
- `--compact` compacts the external conversation.
- `--approve` or `--deny` resolves a pending `@run` permission.
- `--new` creates an external session; `--toggle-plan` toggles read-only mode.
- `--models [NAME]`, `--sessions [ID|NUMBER]`, `--skills [default|on:ID|off:ID|ID,ID]` and `--subagents [NUMBER]` inspect or select those resources.

The agent never runs an outside/project or system command without approval. Inside-project commands run directly. Review the command and its reason before using `--approve`.

## Safety and local data

Model directives are confined to the working folder. `@write` saves a backup before replacing a file; editing Burton's own code is blocked by default. Still, review changes and commands before approving them: a local model can generate incorrect code.

Configuration, sessions, backups, terminal history and worker state are local data and should not be published.

## Tests

From the Burton folder:

```powershell
py -3.12 -m unittest discover -s tests -v
```
